  "text": "Nearly every analyst loop has a spreadsheet round, because that's where quick analysis still happens. The questions are rarely about menus. They're about what a formula does when you copy it, sort it, or point it at the wrong column. Eighteen questions. We'll work the first ten on one small sheet of orders and a product list, then take the last eight a formula at a time. First, the tool that answers a lot of them with no formula at all.\n\nQuestion one. What is a pivot table, and when do you use one? It summarizes a flat table: it groups the rows into categories and aggregates the values. It's GROUP BY that you can drag. Rows and columns hold the groups, values hold the sum, count or average, and filters narrow it down. Use it to explore, or to cross-tab in seconds. Question two. How would you build revenue by region and month? Region in rows, month in columns, revenue in values, summed. North in March is two ninety. It's the same matrix that sum of case when builds in SQL. Switch the summary to count, and that cell says two: two orders made up the two ninety. Most of what comes next is about finding one value in another table.",
